item.text:
The filesystem write was blocked by the read-only sandbox, so I’m including the cumulative manifest below for deterministic merge. No score was assigned.

Research wave summary:

- Documents discovered: ~40
- Promising documents opened: 27
- Accepted new evidence units: 10
- Reused evidence units: 12
- Rejected: 8
- Access-blocked: 5
- Organizations represented: Presidency of Mexico, CONEVAL, IMF, OECD, ASF, SIDOF/Chamber of Deputies, WOLA, CSIS, Integralia, Washington Post
- Source types: official records, audits/budget documents, international organizations, NGO analysis, think-tank analysis, journalism, statistical records

Lens coverage: all ten lenses have evidence. Residual gaps concern direct evidence of individual appointment quality, internal bureaucratic discipline, and documented mid-course correction. These are covered only indirectly through implementation outputs, institutional control, and policy continuity.

A further Chapter 8B-specific search wave would have limited marginal value: the main program, budget, security, militarization, crisis-management, and outcome evidence is reasonably saturated, though appointment-level evidence could improve 8B.4–8B.5.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Cuarto Informe de Gobierno: program and infrastructure implementation","publisher":"Presidency of Mexico","publication_date":"2022-09-01","url":"https://amlo.presidente.gob.mx/informes-de-gobierno/","claim":"The administration reported concrete implementation of its governing program, including direct school-maintenance funding, 200 Benito Juárez universities, 2,750 Banco del Bienestar branches, and 174,000 schools receiving direct maintenance funds.","locator":"Cuarto Informe de Gobierno page, lines 693-695 and 957-978","provisional_id":"WEB-8B-001","canonical_fact_key":"https://amlo.presidente.gob.mx/informes-de-gobierno/|Cuarto Informe lines 693-978|program implementation","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["8B"],"methodology_ids":["8B.1","8B.2","8B.3","8B.6","8B.10"],"source_type":"official_government_report","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Primary administration report with concrete numerical implementation claims, but self-reported.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Reports implementation through the fourth year and specifically in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"The report attributes the programs and infrastructure directly to López Obrador’s administration.","contrary_evidence":["Claims are not independently audited in this source.","Coverage and quality of implementation remain uneven across regions."],"lenses":["8B.1","8B.2","8B.3","8B.6","8B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Resumen integral del desempeño de los programas sociales 2022-2023","publisher":"CONEVAL","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www.coneval.org.mx/EvaluacionDS/PP/Programas/Integral_Desempeno/Paginas/Resumen_Desempeno_2022.aspx","claim":"CONEVAL evaluated 121 federal social programs and actions using standardized monitoring and evaluation information for fiscal year 2022, explicitly identifying progress and areas requiring improvement.","locator":"Summary page, lines 128-135","provisional_id":"WEB-8B-002","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.coneval.org.mx/EvaluacionDS/PP/Programas/Integral_Desempeno/Paginas/Resumen_Desempeno_2022.aspx|lines 128-135|2022 program evaluation","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["8B"],"methodology_ids":["8B.2","8B.6","8B.7","8B.8"],"source_type":"official_independent_evaluation","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"CONEVAL is Mexico’s statutory social-policy evaluation institution and describes a standardized dataset covering 121 programs.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly evaluates fiscal-year 2022 programs.","ruler_attribution":"The evidence concerns programs created, funded, or administered under the López Obrador government, but does not establish personal control over every program result.","contrary_evidence":["The summary page does not itself establish that all programs met objectives.","The page identifies continuing areas of opportunity."],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.6","8B.7","8B.8"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexico: 2022 Article IV Consultation","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-10-18","url":"https://www.imf.org/-/media/files/publications/cr/2022/english/1mexea2022001.pdf","claim":"The administration’s policy agenda focused on redistribution, trade promotion, infrastructure investment, and trade integration, especially in southern states, but the IMF identified persistent shortfalls in education, health, public investment, social safety nets, and program efficiency.","locator":"PDF p. 27, paragraphs 46-48, lines 1368-1403","provisional_id":"WEB-8B-003","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.imf.org/-/media/files/publications/cr/2022/english/1mexea2022001.pdf|p27 paras 46-48|policy agenda and implementation constraints","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["8B"],"methodology_ids":["8B.1","8B.2","8B.6","8B.7","8B.8","8B.10"],"source_type":"international_financial_institution_report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent IMF staff assessment with explicit policy and spending analysis.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published during and assessing the 2022 period.","ruler_attribution":"Attributes the policy agenda to the current administration while distinguishing inherited structural and pandemic constraints.","contrary_evidence":["The report states that major spending and efficiency gaps remained.","It recommends additional reforms rather than describing full achievement."],"lenses":["8B.1","8B.2","8B.6","8B.7","8B.8","8B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Decreto relativo a la revisión de la Cuenta Pública Federal 2022","publisher":"Secretaría de Gobernación / Cámara de Diputados","publication_date":"2025-01-13","url":"https://sidof.segob.gob.mx/notas/docFuente/5749268","claim":"In 2022 total public-sector spending rose 5.1% in real terms; spending exceeded approved amounts notably in Energy, Tourism, Finance, Defense, Education, and Environment, while the audit process identified 32.894 billion pesos in recoveries or amounts requiring clarification.","locator":"Official Gazette text, clauses Segundo through Séptimo, lines 6-25","provisional_id":"WEB-8B-004","canonical_fact_key":"https://sidof.segob.gob.mx/notas/docFuente/5749268|clauses II-VII|2022 expenditure and audit findings","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["8B"],"methodology_ids":["8B.2","8B.3","8B.5","8B.6"],"source_type":"official_budget_and_audit_record","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official public-account review with fiscal totals and audit findings.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly reports execution of the 2022 public budget.","ruler_attribution":"The executive controlled the submitted budget and spending priorities, while individual irregularities may belong to subordinate entities or federalized spending units.","contrary_evidence":["Audit recoveries and clarification requirements indicate implementation and control imperfections.","Overspending relative to approval does not by itself establish policy success."],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.3","8B.5","8B.6"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Informe de auditorías de la Cuenta Pública 2022","publisher":"Auditoría Superior de la Federación","publication_date":"2024","url":"https://www.asf.gob.mx/Trans/Informes/IR2022c/index.html","claim":"The Supreme Audit Institution published numerous 2022 audits covering flagship projects including the Tren Maya, Dos Bocas refinery, Pemex, CFE, and social and research institutions, demonstrating extensive formal oversight of the administration’s implementation apparatus.","locator":"Audit index, lines 32-38 and 275-337","provisional_id":"WEB-8B-005","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.asf.gob.mx/Trans/Informes/IR2022c/index.html|lines 275-337|flagship project audits","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["8B"],"methodology_ids":["8B.2","8B.5","8B.6","8B.8"],"source_type":"supreme_audit_institution_index","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary constitutional audit institution listing project-specific reviews.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Audits fiscal-year 2022 implementation and projects active in that period.","ruler_attribution":"Flagship projects were presidential priorities, although audit findings cannot automatically be attributed personally to López Obrador.","contrary_evidence":["The index establishes scrutiny, not successful implementation.","Several projects were subject to compliance and physical-investment audits, indicating execution risk."],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.5","8B.6","8B.8"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexico Deepens Militarization. But Facts Show it is a Failed Strategy","publisher":"Washington Office on Latin America","publication_date":"2022-09-02","url":"https://www.wola.org/analysis/mexico-deepens-militarization-but-facts-show-failed-strategy/","claim":"López Obrador moved from describing military deployment as temporary toward a durable model placing the National Guard under Defense Ministry control and expanding military control over migration, ports, customs, and infrastructure, while territorial presence had not produced a major reduction in insecurity.","locator":"Analysis sections 2-4, paragraphs corresponding to lines 90-101","provisional_id":"WEB-8B-006","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.wola.org/analysis/mexico-deepens-militarization-but-facts-show-failed-strategy/|sections 2-4|durable militarization strategy","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["8B"],"methodology_ids":["8B.1","8B.2","8B.3","8B.5","8B.6","8B.7","8B.9","8B.10"],"source_type":"NGO_policy_analysis","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Specialist regional human-rights organization offering a detailed contemporary policy analysis; interpretive rather than official.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published during the August-September 2022 policy shift.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributes the proposed institutional change and strategic direction to López Obrador.","contrary_evidence":["The government presented militarization as professionalization and security improvement.","WOLA’s assessment is critical and interpretive."],"lenses":["8B.1","8B.2","8B.3","8B.5","8B.6","8B.7","8B.9","8B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The Political Implications of Mexico’s New Militarism","publisher":"Center for Strategic and International Studies","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/political-implications-mexicos-new-militarism","claim":"CSIS characterized the administration’s militarization as a political undertaking that expanded military responsibilities and contracts while weakening civilian oversight, transparency, and the separation between state, government, and ruling party.","locator":"Analysis lines 254-270","provisional_id":"WEB-8B-007","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/political-implications-mexicos-new-militarism|lines 254-270|militarization as political undertaking","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["8B"],"methodology_ids":["8B.1","8B.3","8B.4","8B.5","8B.9","8B.10"],"source_type":"think_tank_analysis","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Expert policy analysis with explicit institutional interpretation and contemporary evidence references.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Assesses developments initiated and consolidated during the López Obrador administration, including 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributes the expansion of military roles and political alignment to the administration’s strategic choices.","contrary_evidence":["The government argued that the armed forces were trusted and more capable than discredited civilian policing institutions.","The source warns about future risks that may not yet have materialized by end-2022."],"lenses":["8B.1","8B.3","8B.4","8B.5","8B.9","8B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Four Years in Office: 2018-2022","publisher":"Integralia Consultores","publication_date":"2022-12-01","url":"https://integralia.com.mx/web/wp-content/uploads/2022/12/Special-Report_AMLO_Four-years-in-office.pdf","claim":"Using SESNSP, INEGI, CONAPO, México Evalúa, UDLAP, WJP, SEGOB, and media data, Integralia reported that intentional homicide rates fell from 22.4 per 100,000 in 2018 to 20.1 in 2022, while extortion and retail drug dealing increased and impunity remained high.","locator":"PDF pp. 7-10, especially lines 574-639 and 690-694","provisional_id":"WEB-8B-008","canonical_fact_key":"https://integralia.com.mx/web/wp-content/uploads/2022/12/Special-Report_AMLO_Four-years-in-office.pdf|pp7-10|security outcome balance 2018-2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["8B"],"methodology_ids":["8B.7","8B.9","8B.10"],"source_type":"independent_data_analysis","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Named-source comparative analysis using official and independent datasets.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly compares the start of the administration with 2022.","ruler_attribution":"The indicators assess the period under López Obrador but cannot isolate presidential causation from state, municipal, inherited, or criminal factors.","contrary_evidence":["Some security indicators improved while extortion, retail drug dealing, and impunity remained adverse.","The source is a four-year assessment rather than a causal evaluation."],"lenses":["8B.7","8B.9","8B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexico putting civilian-led National Guard under military control","publisher":"The Washington Post","publication_date":"2022-09-09","url":"https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/2022/09/09/mexico-national-guard-army/","claim":"Mexico’s Senate approved putting the 115,000-member National Guard under army control, abandoning the planned civilian-led model; human-rights groups and analysts argued this weakened democratic oversight without addressing the dysfunctional justice system.","locator":"Article opening, paragraphs 1-3, lines 9-24","provisional_id":"WEB-8B-009","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/2022/09/09/mexico-national-guard-army/|opening paragraphs|National Guard transfer and criticism","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["8B"],"methodology_ids":["8B.2","8B.3","8B.5","8B.6","8B.9"],"source_type":"journalism","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporary reporting of a completed legislative action with attributed expert and civil-society criticism.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly reports the September 2022 institutional transfer.","ruler_attribution":"The measure followed López Obrador’s announced strategy and administration-backed legislation, though Senate approval was a separate institutional act.","contrary_evidence":["The government and supporters framed the change as necessary for security and professionalization.","The article reports criticism that the justice-system problem remained unresolved."],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.3","8B.5","8B.6","8B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexico: A comprehensive reform agenda would help optimise the strength and quality of the recovery","publisher":"OECD","publication_date":"2022-02-21","url":"https://www.oecd.org/en/about/news/press-releases/2022/02/mexico-a-comprehensive-reform-agenda-would-help-optimise-the-strength-and-quality-of-the-recovery-and-medium-term-growth.html","claim":"The OECD found that sound macroeconomic management helped Mexico recover from the pandemic and retain market access, but recommended a broader reform agenda, stronger social spending, public investment, productivity reforms, and action on informality and corruption.","locator":"OECD press release, lines 3681-3689","provisional_id":"WEB-8B-010","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.oecd.org/en/about/news/press-releases/2022/02/mexico-a-comprehensive-reform-agenda-would-help-optimise-the-strength-and-quality-of-the-recovery-and-medium-term-growth.html|lines 3681-3689|recovery and unfinished reform agenda","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["8B"],"methodology_ids":["8B.1","8B.2","8B.6","8B.7","8B.8","8B.10"],"source_type":"international_organization_assessment","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent OECD assessment presented with Mexican finance officials and specific policy recommendations.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published in February 2022 and evaluates the administration’s active policy environment.","ruler_attribution":"Attributes macroeconomic management and policy agenda to the administration while identifying structural constraints and unfinished reforms.","contrary_evidence":["The OECD’s positive macroeconomic assessment does not establish achievement of the broader reform program.","It identifies low productivity, informality, corruption, and spending gaps as continuing problems."],"lenses":["8B.1","8B.2","8B.6","8B.7","8B.8","8B.10"]}

Cumulative manifest note: prior entries AMLO22-R01 through AMLO22-R12 remain unchanged and should be retained, followed by WEB-8B-001 through WEB-8B-010.
